%N A399179 The sequence of binary decisions that defines the lexicographically earliest infinite path in the base-10 comma child graph.
%C A399179 We know from König's Infinity Lemma that at least one infinite path exists in the base-10 child graph (see the Angelini et al. (2024) paper for details). This path is defined by an infinite sequence of binary decisions, but at present we only know the first 30 decisions for the lexicographically earliest path. It would be nice to know more terms.
%C A399179 The analogous sequence in the base-3 case is simply 0,1,0,1,0,1,... It is conceivable that the present sequence can also be described by a finite automaton.
%C A399179 From _Paul G Roberts_, Sep 22 2026: (Start)
%C A399179 In the base-10 case, an infinite path must have first term 20 (or a descendent of 20).  In the base-3 case, an infinite path must have first term 1 (or a descendent of 1).
%C A399179 A computer search followed all paths through the base-10 comma sequence having first term 20, to a depth of 10000 branches. All remaining paths that had not been shown to terminate began with the same 9948 decisions. Any infinite path must take those decisions. They are an apparently repeated cycle of 314 decisions, with each cycle advancing the comma sequence by 924 orders of magnitude. (End)
%C A399179 This has now been shown to be the branch sequence of the unique infinite path in the base-10 child graph, and it is indeed periodic with a period of 314. - _Chris Randall_, Sep 24 2026
